    "content": "# Surah Al-Baqarah (البقرة) — Part 7 of 10: Verses 215–242\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah Al-Baqarah (The Cow)**, covering **Verses 215–242**. This lesson is part of the comprehensive FAHMRISE Qur'an Tafsir curriculum, designed to cultivate deep understanding (Fahm), spiritual elevation (Rise), and principled action.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 2 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة البقرة |\n| **English Meaning** | The Cow |\n| **Lesson Part** | Part 7 of 10 |\n| **Verses Covered** | Verses 215–242 (28 verses) |\n| **Classification** | Madani Revelation |\n| **Thematic Focus** | Social Ordinances: Charity, Marriage, Divorce & Orphan Protection |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Circumstances of Revelation (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nRevealed to reform pre-Islamic Arabian injustices against women and orphans, where men would divorce their wives endlessly to torment them, hoard orphans' inheritance, and treat marriage contracts as arbitrary property transactions.\n\nScholars of Tafsir (including Imam al-Wahidi and Imam as-Suyuti) highlight that grasping the historical backdrop and authentic occasions of revelation provides an essential anchor for correctly interpreting divine intent and extracting universal legal and spiritual principles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 215–221: Charity Priorities, Intoxicants, and Marriage Boundaries\n\nThey ask what to spend: whatever good you spend is for parents, relatives, orphans, the needy, and travelers. You may dislike a thing which is good for you, and love a thing which is bad for you. Intoxicants and gambling contain great sin. Do not marry polytheistic women until they believe; a believing bondwoman is better than an idolatress.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 222–232: Marital Sanctity, Oaths, and the Twofold Divorce\n\nMarital relations; oaths in Allah's name. The four-month limit for oaths of abstention. The three-menstrual waiting period for divorced women. The revolutionary law: divorce is twice! After that, either retain in honor or separate with grace. Prohibition of taking back given dowries.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 233–242: Nursing Rights, Widow Care, and Guarding the Prayers\n\nMothers nurse their children for two complete years; fathers must bear the cost of food and clothing with fairness. Do not forget graciousness between you. Guard strictly your five daily prayers, especially the middle prayer (Salat al-Wusta / 'Asr). Stand before Allah in devout obedience.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances (Gharib al-Qur'an)\n\n- **Al-Khamr wal-Maysir (ٱلْخَمْرِ وَٱلْمَيْسِرِ)**: Intoxicating drinks and games of chance—possessing some worldly benefit but vastly greater spiritual and social harm.\n- **Al-Yatama (ٱلْيَتَٰمَىٰ)**: Orphans—the fatherless minors whose wealth and person must be protected with integrity.\n- **At-Talaqu Marratan (ٱلطَّلَٰقُ مَرَّتَانِ)**: Divorce is twice (revocable)—after which one either retains with kindness or releases with grace.\n- **Tasrihun bi-Ihsan (تَسْرِيحٌۢ بِإِحْسَٰنٍ)**: Releasing, separating, and divorcing with noble dignity, fairness, and generosity.\n- **Hafizu 'ala as-Salawat (حَٰفِظُوا۟ عَلَى ٱلصَّلَوَٰتِ)**: Maintain with utmost care and regularity your prayers, especially the middle prayer ('Asr).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multi-Tier Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah Al-Baqarah transform daily character, classical scholars emphasize contextualizing its guidance across life stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** Be kind and gentle to babies and younger siblings; mothers work very hard feeding and caring for them.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Avoid energy drinks with dubious ingredients, peer bets, and gambling apps; Allah warns that the harm of gambling always destroys lives.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** In marriage or divorce, practice 'Ihsan': never allow personal bitterness to withhold child support, slander former spouses, or weaponize children.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Guard the 'Asr prayer (Salat al-Wusta) with special punctuality, never letting work meetings delay it.**\n2. **Review your spending priorities: ensure parents and immediate kin receive primary financial care.**\n3. **Embody the golden rule of verse 237: 'Wa-la tansawu al-fadla baynakum' (And do not forget graciousness between yourselves).**\n",
    "practicalApplication": "Incorporate Surah Al-Baqarah (Verses 215–242) into your daily reflection and voluntary prayers. Focus upon its central theme: Social Ordinances: Charity, Marriage, Divorce & Orphan Protection. Commit to implementing its practical action steps in your home, studies, and workplace.",
