    "content": "# Surah Al-Baqarah (البقرة) — Part 10 of 10: Verses 261–286\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah Al-Baqarah (The Cow)**, covering **Verses 261–286**. This lesson is part of the comprehensive FAHMRISE Qur'an Tafsir curriculum, designed to cultivate deep understanding (Fahm), spiritual elevation (Rise), and principled action.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 2 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة البقرة |\n| **English Meaning** | The Cow |\n| **Lesson Part** | Part 10 of 10 |\n| **Verses Covered** | Verses 261–286 (26 verses) |\n| **Classification** | Madani Revelation |\n| **Thematic Focus** | Islamic Economics: Sincere Charity, The War on Usury & The Climax Invocations |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Circumstances of Revelation (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nVerses 275–281 were among the very last revelations received by the Prophet ﷺ before his passing, declaring the total, irrevocable prohibition of usury and reminding believers of the final Day when every soul will be fully recompensed without injustice.\n\nScholars of Tafsir (including Imam al-Wahidi and Imam as-Suyuti) highlight that grasping the historical backdrop and authentic occasions of revelation provides an essential anchor for correctly interpreting divine intent and extracting universal legal and spiritual principles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 261–274: The Architecture of Sincere Charity\n\nThe parable of spending: like a seed yielding 700 grains. Kind words and forgiveness are better than charity followed by injury. The parable of the hypocrite's charity: like a smooth rock covered with dust washed bare by heavy rain. Spend from the good things you have earned. Satan threatens you with poverty; Allah promises forgiveness and bounty.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 275–281: The Absolute Prohibition and War on Usury (Riba)\n\nThose who consume interest will stand on Judgment Day like one beaten by Satan into madness, because they claim trade is just like usury. Allah has permitted trade and forbidden usury! Allah destroys interest and gives increase to charities. If you do not give up interest, be warned of a war from Allah and His Messenger. If a debtor is in hardship, grant delay until ease, or write it off as charity.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 282–286: Ayat ad-Dayn and the Climax Supplications (Amanar-Rasul)\n\nWhen you contract a debt for a specified term, write it down! Appoint a scribe to record with justice. Call two male witnesses or one man and two women. The closing verses: the Messenger believes in what was revealed, and so do the believers. 'Our Lord, do not impose blame upon us if we forget or make a mistake... do not burden us beyond what we can bear... pardon us, forgive us, and have mercy upon us; You are our protector, so give us victory over the disbelieving people.'\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances (Gharib al-Qur'an)\n\n- **Kama-thali habbah (كَمَثَلِ حَبَّةٍ)**: Like a single grain producing seven ears, with a hundred grains in each ear—a 700-fold multiplication.\n- **Mann wa-Adha (مَنًّۭا وَلَآ أَذًى)**: Reminding recipients of favors or causing emotional hurt—which completely nullifies the reward of charity.\n- **Ar-Riba (ٱلرِّبَوٰا۟)**: Usury, interest, and predatory lending—unearned increments that exploit the needy and incur war from Allah and His Messenger.\n- **Ayat ad-Dayn (ءَايَةُ ٱلدَّيْنِ)**: The Debt Verse (2:282)—the longest single verse in the Qur'an, codifying documentation, recording, and witnesses.\n- **Amanar-Rasul (ءَامَنَ ٱلرَّسُولُ)**: The Messenger has believed—the two concluding verses (2:285–286) gifted to the Prophet ﷺ during the Mir'aj.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multi-Tier Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah Al-Baqarah transform daily character, classical scholars emphasize contextualizing its guidance across life stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** When you share your toys or give some pocket money to someone who needs it, never tease them or brag about it.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Avoid credit card debt and student interest traps; ethical finance and avoiding Riba protects your future from economic enslavement.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** Implement rigorous financial documentation in business; never rely on verbal promises for loans, and eliminate all interest-bearing investments.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Recite the last two verses of Surah Al-Baqarah (2:285–286) every single night as recommended in Sahih al-Bukhari.**\n2. **Document every personal loan and business contract in writing with two witnesses as commanded in verse 282.**\n3. **Purify your financial accounts from any interest income by donating it to public charity without claiming tax write-offs.**\n",
