    "content": "# Surah An-Nisa (النساء) — Part 1 of 6: Verses 1–23\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah An-Nisa (The Women)**, covering **Verses 1–23**. This lesson is part of the comprehensive FAHMRISE Qur'an Tafsir curriculum, designed to cultivate deep understanding (Fahm), spiritual elevation (Rise), and principled action.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 4 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة النساء |\n| **English Meaning** | The Women |\n| **Lesson Part** | Part 1 of 6 |\n| **Verses Covered** | Verses 1–23 (23 verses) |\n| **Classification** | Madani Revelation |\n| **Thematic Focus** | Human Unity, Orphan Rights & The Divine Mathematics of Inheritance |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Circumstances of Revelation (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nRevealed following the Battle of Uhud when dozens of Muslim men were martyred, leaving numerous widows and vulnerable orphans in Madinah whose guardians often hoarded their inheritances or married them without equitable dowries.\n\nScholars of Tafsir (including Imam al-Wahidi and Imam as-Suyuti) highlight that grasping the historical backdrop and authentic occasions of revelation provides an essential anchor for correctly interpreting divine intent and extracting universal legal and spiritual principles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 1–6: Creation from a Single Soul and Safeguarding Orphans' Wealth\n\nFear your Lord Who created you from one soul and created from it its mate. Revere the wombs. Give orphans their wealth in full; do not substitute bad items for good ones, nor consume their wealth with yours—that is a grave sin. If you fear you will not be just with female orphans, marry two, three, or four; but if you fear injustice, then only one. Test orphans until marriageable age, then release their wealth.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 7–14: The Divine Law of Inheritance (Ilm al-Mawarith)\n\nFor men is a share of what parents and kin leave, and for women is an independent share. When distributing, give charity to present relatives, orphans, and the needy. Those who consume the property of orphans unjustly swallow nothing but fire into their bellies! The precise shares: children (male gets the share of two females), parents (one-sixth each if children exist), spouses (half, fourth, eighth). These are the legal limits of Allah (*Tilka Hududullah*).\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 15–23: Reforming Moral Laws and Prohibited Marital Relations\n\nGradual penalties for early immoralities before final codes. Repentance is accepted from those who commit evil in ignorance and repent soon after. Prohibition of inheriting women against their will or treating them harshly. Prohibited for marriage: mothers, daughters, sisters, aunts, nieces, foster-mothers, foster-sisters, mothers-in-law, and stepdaughters.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances (Gharib al-Qur'an)\n\n- **Nafsin Wahidah (نَّفْسٍ وَٰحِدَةٍ)**: A single soul—affirming the unified biological and spiritual origin of all humanity from Adam.\n- **Al-Arham (ٱلْأَرْحَامَ)**: The wombs—the sacred ties of maternal kinship, family bonds, and blood relations.\n- **Al-Yatama (ٱلْيَتَٰمَىٰ)**: The fatherless orphans whose wealth must never be consumed fraudulently or mingled unfairly.\n- **Faridatan mina Allah (فَرِيضَةً مِّنَ ٱللَّهِ)**: An ordained, binding obligation directly from Allah—describing the precise mathematical inheritance shares.\n- **Al-Muhsanat (ٱلْمُحْصَنَٰتُ)**: Chaste, legally married women whose marital sanctity is protected.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multi-Tier Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah An-Nisa transform daily character, classical scholars emphasize contextualizing its guidance across life stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** Always share toys fairly and protect friends who don't have fathers; stealing from an orphan is a very bad sin in Islam.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Honor women as independent human beings with full rights to own money, property, and choices; Islam granted women financial independence 1,400 years ago.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** Implement the divine inheritance laws strictly: never deprive daughters, sisters, or mothers of their Quranic shares based on cultural traditions.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Audit any family estates to ensure women have received their complete Quranic inheritance shares (*Faridah*).**\n2. **Sponsor a vulnerable orphan and ensure their finances are tracked with 100% transparent accounting (4:6).**\n3. **Strengthen ties with maternal relatives to honor the command: 'Wattaqu Allaha alladhi tasa'aluna bihi wal-arham'.**\n",
    "practicalApplication": "Incorporate Surah An-Nisa (Verses 1–23) into your daily reflection and voluntary prayers. Focus upon its central theme: Human Unity, Orphan Rights & The Divine Mathematics of Inheritance. Commit to implementing its practical action steps in your home, studies, and workplace.",
