    "content": "# Surah An-Nisa (النساء) — Part 2 of 6: Verses 24–42\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah An-Nisa (The Women)**, covering **Verses 24–42**. This lesson is part of the comprehensive FAHMRISE Qur'an Tafsir curriculum, designed to cultivate deep understanding (Fahm), spiritual elevation (Rise), and principled action.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 4 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة النساء |\n| **English Meaning** | The Women |\n| **Lesson Part** | Part 2 of 6 |\n| **Verses Covered** | Verses 24–42 (19 verses) |\n| **Classification** | Madani Revelation |\n| **Thematic Focus** | Marital Stewardship, Dispute Arbitration & Universal Kindness |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Circumstances of Revelation (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nRevealed to provide a holistic framework for marital harmony, protect women from financial exploitation, and replace domestic violence with civilized communication and independent mediation.\n\nScholars of Tafsir (including Imam al-Wahidi and Imam as-Suyuti) highlight that grasping the historical backdrop and authentic occasions of revelation provides an essential anchor for correctly interpreting divine intent and extracting universal legal and spiritual principles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 24–33: Lawful Marriage, Mutual Consent, and Financial Stewardship\n\nAll women beyond the prohibited degrees are lawful for marriage seeking chastity, not lust. Do not consume one another's wealth unjustly, but let there be trade by mutual consent. Do not kill yourselves; indeed Allah is to you Merciful. If you avoid the major sins, We will remove your minor misdeeds and admit you to a noble entrance. Do not envy what Allah has favored some over others.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 34–35: Family Leadership and Bilateral Dispute Arbitration\n\nMen are providers and guardians through what Allah favored them and what they spend of wealth. Righteous women are devoutly obedient, guarding in absence what Allah would have them guard. If you fear discord between them, appoint an arbiter from his family and an arbiter from her family; if they desire reconciliation, Allah will cause it between them.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 36–42: The Universal Charter of Social Compassion\n\nWorship Allah and associate nothing with Him, and do good to: (1) parents, (2) relatives, (3) orphans, (4) the needy, (5) the near neighbor, (6) the distant neighbor, (7) the companion at your side, (8) the traveler, and those your right hands possess. Allah does not love the arrogant boaster who is stingy and encourages others to stinginess.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances (Gharib al-Qur'an)\n\n- **Ar-Rijalu Qawwamuna (ٱلرِّجَالُ قَوَّٰمُونَ)**: Men are guardians, protectors, and financial providers responsible for supporting and serving the family.\n- **Qanitat (قَٰنِتَٰتٌ)**: Devoutly obedient to Allah, loyal, righteous, and nurturing partners.\n- **Hakamayn (حَكَمَيْنِ)**: Two impartial arbiters—one from the husband's family and one from the wife's family—seeking sincere reconciliation.\n- **Kaba'ira ma Tunhawna 'anhu (كَبَآئِرَ مَا تُنْهَوْنَ عَنْهُ)**: The major sins prohibited by Allah—avoiding which results in the expiation of minor daily faults.\n- **Al-Jari dhil-Qurba wal-Jari al-Junub (ٱلْجَارِ ذِى ٱلْقُرْبَىٰ وَٱلْجَارِ ٱلْجُنُبِ)**: The near neighbor (close in proximity or relation) and the distant neighbor (physically or religiously).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multi-Tier Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah An-Nisa transform daily character, classical scholars emphasize contextualizing its guidance across life stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** Be extra friendly to the children living next door to your house; our Prophet taught us that neighbors have special rights.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Don't get jealous of other people's looks, grades, or money on Instagram; Allah gives each person unique blessings (4:32).\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** Remember that 'Qawwamun' means responsibility and service, not tyranny; when marital disputes arise, bring wise family elders to mediate early.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Send food or a gift to your nearest neighbor this week to honor the divine commandment of verse 36.**\n2. **Avoid the major sins (Shirk, murder, sorcery, usury, slandering chaste women, desertion in battle, consuming orphan wealth).**\n3. **Whenever marital friction occurs, avoid unilateral anger and apply the arbitration framework of verse 35.**\n",
