    "content": "# Surah An-Nisa (النساء) — Part 4 of 6: Verses 71–104\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah An-Nisa (The Women)**, covering **Verses 71–104**. This lesson is part of the comprehensive FAHMRISE Qur'an Tafsir curriculum, designed to cultivate deep understanding (Fahm), spiritual elevation (Rise), and principled action.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 4 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة النساء |\n| **English Meaning** | The Women |\n| **Lesson Part** | Part 4 of 6 |\n| **Verses Covered** | Verses 71–104 (34 verses) |\n| **Classification** | Madani Revelation |\n| **Thematic Focus** | Defense of the Oppressed, Rules of Blood Money & Salat al-Khawf |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Circumstances of Revelation (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nRevealed during campaigns when Muslim forces faced surprise attacks, addressing companions who asked why they were commanded to fight in defense when earlier in Makkah they were told to restrain their hands, and legislating how to pray in active combat zones without abandoning weapons.\n\nScholars of Tafsir (including Imam al-Wahidi and Imam as-Suyuti) highlight that grasping the historical backdrop and authentic occasions of revelation provides an essential anchor for correctly interpreting divine intent and extracting universal legal and spiritual principles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 71–84: Preparedness, Liberating the Weak, and Rumor Verification\n\nTake your precautions and advance in detachments. Why should you not fight in the cause of Allah for the oppressed men, women, and children who cry: 'Our Lord, bring us out of this city whose people are oppressors and appoint for us a protector!' Satan's plot is ever weak. When information concerning security or fear comes to them, they broadcast it; had they referred it to the Messenger and those in authority, those who extract insights would have understood it.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 85–94: Intercession, Greetings of Peace, and Accidental Manslaughter\n\nWhoever intercedes for a good cause receives a share; whoever intercedes for evil carries a burden. When greeted with a greeting, greet with one better or return it. It is not for a believer to kill a believer except by mistake: the compensation is freeing a believing slave and paying blood money (Diyah) to the family. Whoever kills a believer intentionally: his retribution is Hellfire eternally. When you go forth, investigate thoroughly and do not say to one who offers you peace: 'You are not a believer!'\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 95–104: Degrees of Believers, Migration, and the Fear Prayer\n\nNot equal are the passive sitters and those who strive with wealth and lives. Those who died oppressed while able to migrate are rebuked: 'Was not the earth of Allah spacious enough for you to migrate?' Shortening prayers during travel. The Fear Prayer: one group prays with the Prophet carrying weapons while the other guards; then they switch. When prayer is finished, remember Allah standing, sitting, and lying down. Prayer is an obligation upon believers at specified times.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances (Gharib al-Qur'an)\n\n- **Khudhu Hizrakum (خُذُوا۟ حِذْرَكُمْ)**: Take your precautions, maintain military readiness, and stay vigilant against surprise ambushes.\n- **Al-Mustad'afina (ٱلْمُسْتَضْعَفِينَ)**: The vulnerable, oppressed, and helpless men, women, and children unable to devise a plan or find a way out.\n- **Tabayyanu (تَبَيَّنُوا۟)**: Investigate thoroughly, verify facts, and do not rush to judgment without evidence.\n- **Diyah (دِيَةٌ)**: Blood money—financial restitution paid to the family of a victim of accidental manslaughter.\n- **Salat al-Khawf (صَلَوٰةُ ٱلْخَوْفِ)**: The Prayer of Fear / Danger—where the army divides into two groups, alternating prayer and armed sentry duty.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multi-Tier Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah An-Nisa transform daily character, classical scholars emphasize contextualizing its guidance across life stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** When someone says 'As-salamu alaykum' to you, always smile and say 'Wa alaykum as-salam wa rahmatullahi wa barakatuh' with even more friendliness.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Don't share or retweet rumors and drama before checking if it's true (4:83); spreading unverified stories causes real damage.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** Never abandon prayer due to busy schedules: if soldiers in active battle zones had to pray under artillery fire (Salat al-Khawf), we have zero excuse at our desks.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Apply the golden rule of verse 86: respond to every Islamic greeting with a warmer, more complete formulation.**\n2. **Verify every news headline, forwarded WhatsApp message, or political clip before passing it on to others (4:83).**\n3. **Support humanitarian and legal aid campaigns rescuing oppressed families and refugees fleeing tyranny.**\n",
