    "content": "# Surah An-Nisa (النساء) — Part 5 of 6: Verses 105–134\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah An-Nisa (The Women)**, covering **Verses 105–134**. This lesson is part of the comprehensive FAHMRISE Qur'an Tafsir curriculum, designed to cultivate deep understanding (Fahm), spiritual elevation (Rise), and principled action.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 4 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة النساء |\n| **English Meaning** | The Women |\n| **Lesson Part** | Part 5 of 6 |\n| **Verses Covered** | Verses 105–134 (30 verses) |\n| **Classification** | Madani Revelation |\n| **Thematic Focus** | Judicial Integrity: Defending Truth & The Absolute Standard of Justice |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Circumstances of Revelation (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nTu'mah ibn Ubayriq, a nominal Muslim of the Ansar, stole an armored shield from his neighbor in a sack of flour and hid it at the home of a Jewish merchant named Zayd ibn as-Samin. When discovered by flour trails, Tu'mah's clan swore false oaths before the Prophet ﷺ claiming Tu'mah was innocent and framing the Jew. Allah revealed verses 105–113 to vindicate the Jewish man and severely rebuke anyone defending deceivers.\n\nScholars of Tafsir (including Imam al-Wahidi and Imam as-Suyuti) highlight that grasping the historical backdrop and authentic occasions of revelation provides an essential anchor for correctly interpreting divine intent and extracting universal legal and spiritual principles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 105–115: The Case of the Stolen Shield: Vindication of the Innocent\n\nWe revealed the Book to you in truth that you may judge between people by what Allah has shown you; do not be for the deceitful an advocate. Seek Allah's forgiveness. Whoever commits an offense or sin and then blames it on an innocent person has burdened himself with slander and flagrant guilt. If not for Allah's favor upon you, a group of them would have misled you, but they mislead only themselves.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 116–126: Secret Whispering and the False Promises of Satan\n\nNo good is there in much of their private conversation, except for one who enjoins charity, good conduct, or reconciliation between people. Satan promises them and arouses false desires, but Satan promises nothing except delusion. It will not be in accordance with your desires nor the People of Scripture: whoever does an evil will be recompensed for it. Who is better in religion than one who submits his face to Allah while being a doer of good and follows the creed of Ibrahim?\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 127–134: Rulings on Women, Peaceful Settlement, and Absolute Justice\n\nGuidance concerning female orphans. If a woman fears contempt or desertion from her husband, there is no blame upon them if they make a peaceful settlement, and settlement is best (*As-Sulhu khayr*). You will never be able to be totally equal between wives in emotional inclination, even if you strive, but do not lean completely. If they separate, Allah will enrich each from His abundance.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances (Gharib al-Qur'an)\n\n- **Khasiyma (خَصِيمًا)**: An advocate, defense pleader, or partisan defender arguing on behalf of betrayers.\n- **Buhtanan wa-Ithman Mubina (بُهْتَٰنًا وَإِثْمًا مُّبِينًا)**: A monstrous slander and blatant, manifest sin—incurred by framing an innocent person.\n- **An-Najwa (ٱلنَّجْوَىٰ)**: Private, secret whispering, backroom conferences, and closed-door plotting.\n- **Qawwamina bil-Qist (قَوَّٰمِينَ بِٱلْقِسْطِ)**: Unflinchingly upright for justice—establishing absolute equity without fear, favor, or compromise.\n- **Shuhada'a Lillahi (شُهَدَآءَ لِلَّهِ)**: Witnesses for Allah alone—refusing to skew testimony for family loyalty, national pride, or monetary bribe.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multi-Tier Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah An-Nisa transform daily character, classical scholars emphasize contextualizing its guidance across life stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** Never blame a sibling or classmate for something you broke or messed up; telling the truth even when scared is what makes you brave.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Don't engage in exclusive secret group-chats or whispering behind friends' backs; private whispering is only blessed when helping people or fixing fights.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** Study the Tu'mah trial: Islamic courts established 1,400 years ago that a Jewish citizen's innocence must be defended against a Muslim perpetrator with total impartiality.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Adopt the standard of verse 114: never initiate or participate in secret conversations unless promoting charity, virtue, or peace between rivals.**\n2. **Check your testimony in any legal or workplace inquiry: never protect a guilty friend or coworker to save face.**\n3. **Live by the motto: 'As-Sulhu khayr' (Peaceful reconciliation is always best) in family and business conflicts.**\n",
    "practicalApplication": "Incorporate Surah An-Nisa (Verses 105–134) into your daily reflection and voluntary prayers. Focus upon its central theme: Judicial Integrity: Defending Truth & The Absolute Standard of Justice. Commit to implementing its practical action steps in your home, studies, and workplace.",
