    "content": "# Surah An-Nisa (النساء) — Part 6 of 6: Verses 135–176\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah An-Nisa (The Women)**, covering **Verses 135–176**. This lesson is part of the comprehensive FAHMRISE Qur'an Tafsir curriculum, designed to cultivate deep understanding (Fahm), spiritual elevation (Rise), and principled action.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 4 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة النساء |\n| **English Meaning** | The Women |\n| **Lesson Part** | Part 6 of 6 |\n| **Verses Covered** | Verses 135–176 (42 verses) |\n| **Classification** | Madani Revelation |\n| **Thematic Focus** | Standing for Justice, Refutation of the Crucifixion & Kalalah |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Circumstances of Revelation (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nRevealed addressing both hypocrites who oscillated between camps depending on who had the upper hand, and Christian and Jewish theological claims regarding the fate and nature of Prophet 'Isa.\n\nScholars of Tafsir (including Imam al-Wahidi and Imam as-Suyuti) highlight that grasping the historical backdrop and authentic occasions of revelation provides an essential anchor for correctly interpreting divine intent and extracting universal legal and spiritual principles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 135–147: Stand Firm for Justice and the Anatomy of Hypocrites\n\n'O you who believe, be persistently standing firm in justice, witnesses for Allah, even if it be against yourselves or parents and relatives. Whether one is rich or poor, Allah is more worthy of both. So follow not personal inclination, lest you not be just.' Warning: hypocrites sit on the fence; they wait to see who wins. When they stand for prayer, they stand lazily, to be seen of men. The hypocrites will be in the lowest depths of the Fire—unless they repent and purify their faith.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 148–159: Speaking Bad Aloud, Past Sins, and Refuting the Crucifixion\n\nAllah does not like the public utterance of bad words except by one who has been wronged. Sins of past generations: breaking covenants, killing prophets, claiming their hearts are wrapped. Their arrogant boast: 'We killed the Messiah, 'Isa son of Maryam.' But they killed him not, nor did they crucify him; rather, it was made to appear so to them. Those who differ over it are in doubt with no knowledge but following conjecture. And they did not kill him, for certain! Rather, Allah raised him to Himself.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 160–176: Prophetic Revelation, Divine Speech with Musa, and Kalalah Inheritance\n\nWe revealed to you as We revealed to Nuh and the prophets after him. And to Musa Allah spoke directly (*Taklima*). Messengers bringing glad tidings and warnings so that humanity would have no argument against Allah after the messengers. O People of Scripture, do not exceed limits in your religion and do not say of Allah except the truth: the Messiah is only a messenger of Allah and His Word. Concluding verse 176: precise inheritance shares for siblings in the case of Kalalah.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances (Gharib al-Qur'an)\n\n- **Shuhada'a bil-Qist (شُهَدَآءَ بِٱلْقِسْطِ)**: Witnesses for justice—refusing to let emotional affection, blood loyalty, or financial bias bend the truth.\n- **Mudhabdhabina bayna dhalik (مُّذَبْذَبِينَ بَيْنَ ذَٰلِكَ)**: Wavering, oscillating between the two camps—belonging neither to the believers nor to the disbelievers.\n- **Ad-Dark al-Asfal (ٱلدَّرْكِ ٱلْأَسْفَلِ)**: The lowest, most agonizing subterranean abyss of Hellfire reserved for unrepentant hypocrites.\n- **Shubbiha lahum (شُبِّهَ لَهُمْ)**: 'It was made to appear so unto them'—the true fate of 'Isa, whom Allah raised unto Himself.\n- **Al-Kalalah (ٱلْكَلَٰلَةِ)**: A deceased person who leaves behind neither parents (ascendants) nor children (descendants).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multi-Tier Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah An-Nisa transform daily character, classical scholars emphasize contextualizing its guidance across life stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** Always be honest even if you might get into trouble; Allah loves someone who tells the truth against themselves.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Don't pray lazily or just because people are watching (4:142); pray because you love talking to the Creator of the universe.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** Embody verse 135: never bend court testimony, business evaluations, or contract reviews to favor your wealthy brother or pity your poor cousin.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Memorize verse 135 as your professional and personal charter of uncompromising ethical integrity.**\n2. **Check your prayers for signs of hypocrisy: pray with energy, focus, and devotion when completely alone.**\n3. **Affirm the true prophetic status of 'Isa (Jesus) as a beloved, noble messenger of Allah raised alive to the heavens.**\n",
