    "content": "# Surah Al-Ma'idah (المائدة) — Part 4 of 5: Verses 51–86\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah Al-Ma'idah (The Table Spread)**, covering **Verses 51–86**. This lesson is part of the comprehensive FAHMRISE Qur'an Tafsir curriculum, designed to cultivate deep understanding (Fahm), spiritual elevation (Rise), and principled action.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 5 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة المائدة |\n| **English Meaning** | The Table Spread |\n| **Lesson Part** | Part 4 of 5 |\n| **Verses Covered** | Verses 51–86 (36 verses) |\n| **Classification** | Madani Revelation |\n| **Thematic Focus** | Alliances of Faith, The Prohibition of Alcohol & Broken Oaths |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Circumstances of Revelation (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nVerses 90–91 were revealed definitively prohibiting alcohol and gambling after earlier partial verses (2:219 and 4:43), when companions experienced brawls and slurred speech during gatherings.\n\nScholars of Tafsir (including Imam al-Wahidi and Imam as-Suyuti) highlight that grasping the historical backdrop and authentic occasions of revelation provides an essential anchor for correctly interpreting divine intent and extracting universal legal and spiritual principles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 51–56: Alliances of Faith and the Divine Replacement Promise\n\nDo not take those who mock your faith as protectors. If any among you turns back from his religion, Allah will bring forth a people whom He loves and who love Him—gentle toward believers, firm against opponents, striving in the path of Allah, and fearing not the blame of any critic. Your true allies are Allah, His Messenger, and the believers.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 57–86: The Final Prohibition of Intoxicants and Expiation for Oaths\n\nMockery of the call to prayer (Adhan). O believers, intoxicants (Khamr), gambling (Maysir), idol stones, and divination arrows are an abomination of Satan; avoid them so that you may succeed. Satan only seeks to cause animosity and hatred through alcohol and gambling and distract you from prayer. Will you not then desist? Expiation for broken oaths. The tearful sincerity of righteous Christian scholars upon hearing revelation.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances (Gharib al-Qur'an)\n\n- **Yuhibbuhum wa-yuhibbunahu (يُحِبُّهُمْ وَيُحِبُّونَهُۥٓ)**: 'He loves them and they love Him'—humble toward believers, firm against oppressors, striving in Allah's path without fear of blame.\n- **Rijsun min 'amali ash-Shaytan (رِجْسٌ مِّنْ عَمَلِ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنِ)**: An abomination and defilement of Satan's handiwork—describing intoxicants and gambling.\n- **Fa-htanibuh (فَٱجْتَنِبُوهُ)**: 'So avoid it completely!'—the strongest linguistic formula of categorical prohibition in Arabic.\n- **Kaffarat al-Yameen (كَفَّٰرَتُهُۥ)**: Expiation for broken oaths: feeding ten poor people, clothing them, freeing an enslaved person, or fasting three days.\n- **Qissisina wa-Ruhbana (قِسِّيسِينَ وَرُهْبَانًا)**: Priests and monks who are not arrogant, whose eyes overflow with tears upon hearing the truth.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multi-Tier Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah Al-Ma'idah transform daily character, classical scholars emphasize contextualizing its guidance across life stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** Stay away from alcoholic drinks and games where people bet money; they make people lose their minds, get angry, and fight.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Don't let anyone peer-pressure you into drinking, vaping, or gambling; having the courage to say 'No' is true strength.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** Fulfill your oaths seriously: if you swear an oath and must break it for a better alternative, fulfill the Kaffarah (feeding ten poor people) immediately (5:89).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Ensure your home and social gatherings are 100% free from all alcohol and gambling activities (5:90).**\n2. **If you have broken an oath in Allah's name, feed ten needy people (or provide basic meals) to discharge the Kaffarah.**\n3. **Cultivate the qualities of the beloved generation (5:54): humility toward fellow believers, courage for truth, and freedom from social intimidation.**\n",
