    "content": "# Surah Al-Ma'idah (المائدة) — Part 5 of 5: Verses 87–120\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah Al-Ma'idah (The Table Spread)**, covering **Verses 87–120**. This lesson is part of the comprehensive FAHMRISE Qur'an Tafsir curriculum, designed to cultivate deep understanding (Fahm), spiritual elevation (Rise), and principled action.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 5 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة المائدة |\n| **English Meaning** | The Table Spread |\n| **Lesson Part** | Part 5 of 5 |\n| **Verses Covered** | Verses 87–120 (34 verses) |\n| **Classification** | Madani Revelation |\n| **Thematic Focus** | The Heavenly Table Spread & The Solemn Testimony of 'Isa |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Circumstances of Revelation (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nRevealed when some companions considered castrating themselves or giving up meat and marriage to live like monks, prompting verse 87. Verses 112–115 recount the miracle requested by the Apostles (Al-Hawariyyun) for a heavenly feast.\n\nScholars of Tafsir (including Imam al-Wahidi and Imam as-Suyuti) highlight that grasping the historical backdrop and authentic occasions of revelation provides an essential anchor for correctly interpreting divine intent and extracting universal legal and spiritual principles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 87–100: Moderate Asceticism and the Sanctity of Makkah\n\nDo not forbid the wholesome things Allah made lawful for you, and do not transgress. Hunting rules in Ihram. Allah made the Ka'bah, the Sacred House, an asylum of stability for mankind. Not equal are the evil and the good, even if the abundance of evil impresses you; so fear Allah, O people of understanding.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 101–115: Unnecessary Questions, Wills in Travel, and the Table Spread\n\nDo not ask about things which, if made plain, would trouble you. Taking two witnesses for wills during travel. The Apostles ask 'Isa: 'Can your Lord send down to us a table spread from heaven?' 'Isa prays: 'O Allah, send down to us a table spread from the sky to be for us a festival (Eid) and a sign.' Allah promises to send it, warning that anyone who disbelieves after witnessing it faces severe retribution.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 116–120: The Climax Cross-Examination of 'Isa on Judgment Day\n\nAllah asks 'Isa: 'Did you say to the people: Take me and my mother as deities besides Allah?' 'Isa responds in profound reverence: 'Glory be to You! It was not for me to say what I had no right to say. If I had said it, You would have known it. I said not to them except what You commanded me: to worship Allah, my Lord and your Lord. If You punish them, indeed they are Your servants; and if You forgive them, You are the Exalted in Might, the Wise.' Allah affirms: 'This is the Day when the truthful benefit from their truthfulness.'\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances (Gharib al-Qur'an)\n\n- **Al-Ma'idah (ٱلْمَآئِدَةَ)**: The served, heavenly dining table spread laden with miraculous food requested as a sign.\n- **Al-Hawariyyun (ٱلْحَوَارِيُّونَ)**: The sincere disciples and core companions of Prophet 'Isa who affirmed faith.\n- **Kunta anta ar-Raqiba (كُنتَ أَنتَ ٱلرَّقِيبَ)**: 'You were the Watchful Observer over them'—words of 'Isa to Allah on Judgment Day.\n- **In tu'adhdhibhum (إِن تُعَذِّبْهُمْ)**: 'If You punish them, indeed they are Your servants; and if You forgive them, You are the Exalted in Might, Wise.'\n- **Yawmu yanfa'u as-Sadiqin (يَوْمُ يَنفَعُ ٱلصَّٰدِقِينَ)**: 'This is the Day when the truthful will benefit from their truthfulness.'\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multi-Tier Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah Al-Ma'idah transform daily character, classical scholars emphasize contextualizing its guidance across life stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** Islam is balanced: we enjoy good food, family, and play, while always praying and thanking Allah for His gifts.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Don't follow trends just because they are popular; verse 100 reminds us that bad things are still bad even if everyone is doing them.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** Contemplate the profound humility of Prophet 'Isa in verses 116–118: no human, no matter how holy, shares in the exclusive sovereignty of God.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Avoid extreme asceticism: enjoy lawful provisions with gratitude and avoid declaring Halal things Haram (5:87).**\n2. **Adopt the critical moral filter of verse 100: evaluate choices by quality and righteousness, never by popularity.**\n3. **Recite the profound supplication of Prophet 'Isa (5:118) in your night prayers: 'In tu'adhdhibhum fa-innahum 'ibaduk, wa-in taghfir lahum fa-innaka anta al-'Azizu al-Hakim'.**\n",
    "practicalApplication": "Incorporate Surah Al-Ma'idah (Verses 87–120) into your daily reflection and voluntary prayers. Focus upon its central theme: The Heavenly Table Spread & The Solemn Testimony of 'Isa. Commit to implementing its practical action steps in your home, studies, and workplace.",
