    "content": "# Surah Al-An'am (الأنعام) — Part 1 of 5: Verses 1–35\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah Al-An'am (The Cattle)**, covering **Verses 1–35**. This lesson is part of the comprehensive FAHMRISE Qur'an Tafsir curriculum, designed to cultivate deep understanding (Fahm), spiritual elevation (Rise), and principled action.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 6 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة الأنعام |\n| **English Meaning** | The Cattle |\n| **Lesson Part** | Part 1 of 5 |\n| **Verses Covered** | Verses 1–35 (35 verses) |\n| **Classification** | Makki Revelation |\n| **Thematic Focus** | The Symphony of Tawheed, Creation & Prophetic Consolation |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Circumstances of Revelation (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nSurah Al-An'am was revealed in Makkah all at once during a single night, escorted by 70,000 angels glorifying Allah, to establish the bedrock foundations of Islamic creed against polytheism.\n\nScholars of Tafsir (including Imam al-Wahidi and Imam as-Suyuti) highlight that grasping the historical backdrop and authentic occasions of revelation provides an essential anchor for correctly interpreting divine intent and extracting universal legal and spiritual principles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 1–11: The Creator of Darkness and Light, and the Fate of Mockers\n\nPraise belongs to Allah Who created the heavens and earth. Yet those who disbelieve hold others as equal to their Lord. He created you from clay and appointed a term. Travel through the land and see the end of those who denied the messengers.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 12–18: The Decree of Mercy and the Supreme Subduer\n\nTo whom belongs whatever is in the heavens and earth? Say: To Allah. He has decreed upon Himself mercy. If Allah touches you with adversity, none can remove it but He; and if He touches you with good, He is over all things competent. He is the Subduer over His servants.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 19–35: The Greatest Testimony and Consolation of the Messenger\n\nWhat thing is greatest in testimony? Say: Allah is witness between me and you. This Qur'an was revealed that I may warn you and whoever it reaches. We know that what they say grieves you; indeed they do not call you a liar, but the wrongdoers deny Allah's signs. Messengers before you were patient under persecution until victory came.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances (Gharib al-Qur'an)\n\n- **Az-Zulumati wan-Nur (ٱلظُّلُمَٰتِ وَٱلنُّورَ)**: Darknesses (plural—representing all forms of falsehood) and Light (singular—the unified truth of Tawheed).\n- **Kataba 'ala nafsihi ar-Rahmah (كَتَبَ عَلَىٰ نَفْسِهِ ٱلرَّحْمَةَ)**: 'He has decreed upon Himself mercy'—the foundational principle of divine grace over wrath.\n- **Al-Qahir (ٱلْقَاهِرُ)**: The Irresistible Subduer—Who has absolute supreme dominion over all His creation.\n- **La yukadhdhibunaka (لَا يُكَذِّبُونَكَ)**: 'They do not accuse you of lying'—they knew the Prophet was Al-Amin (the trustworthy) but rejected out of pride.\n- **Tawallaw (تَوَلَّوْا۟)**: They turned away obstinately, blinded by arrogance and worldly attachment.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multi-Tier Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah Al-An'am transform daily character, classical scholars emphasize contextualizing its guidance across life stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** Allah created the sun, the moon, and our earth; whenever you feel sad or left out, remember that Allah is always with you.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** When people mock you or leave mean comments for doing what is right, remember that truth-tellers throughout history faced the same; keep your head high.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** Find peace in the divine principle: 'Kataba 'ala nafsihi ar-Rahmah'—Allah's mercy encompasses all who turn to Him with sincere repentance.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Reflect on verse 12 upon waking: praise Allah Who wrote mercy upon His own divine nature.**\n2. **Travel or walk through nature with contemplative eyes as commanded in verse 11 ('Siru fil-ard').**\n3. **Persevere with prophetic patience when facing social friction or misunderstanding due to Islamic practice.**\n",
