    "content": "# Surah Al-An'am (الأنعام) — Part 3 of 5: Verses 74–90\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah Al-An'am (The Cattle)**, covering **Verses 74–90**. This lesson is part of the comprehensive FAHMRISE Qur'an Tafsir curriculum, designed to cultivate deep understanding (Fahm), spiritual elevation (Rise), and principled action.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 6 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة الأنعام |\n| **English Meaning** | The Cattle |\n| **Lesson Part** | Part 3 of 5 |\n| **Verses Covered** | Verses 74–90 (17 verses) |\n| **Classification** | Makki Revelation |\n| **Thematic Focus** | Prophet Ibrahim's Celestial Logic & The 18 Prophets of Guidance |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Circumstances of Revelation (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nRevealed to equip the Prophet ﷺ and early Muslims with rational, theological arguments against both idol worshippers in Makkah and Sabian star-worshippers in Arabia.\n\nScholars of Tafsir (including Imam al-Wahidi and Imam as-Suyuti) highlight that grasping the historical backdrop and authentic occasions of revelation provides an essential anchor for correctly interpreting divine intent and extracting universal legal and spiritual principles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 74–79: Ibrahim's Observation of the Stars, Moon, and Sun\n\nIbrahim challenges his father Azar over taking carved idols as deities. Allah shows Ibrahim the kingdoms of the heavens and earth. When night covers him, he sees a star: 'This is my lord?' When it sets, he says: 'I do not love those that set.' He sees the rising moon: when it sets, he says: 'Unless my Lord guides me, I will be among the straying.' He sees the rising sun, radiant and larger: 'This is my lord; this is greater.' When it sets, he proclaims: 'O my people, I am free of what you associate! I have turned my face toward the Originator of the heavens and earth!'\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 80–83: The Argument for True Security and the Meaning of Zulm\n\nHis people dispute with him; he asks: 'Do you argue with me concerning Allah when He has guided me? Which of the two parties has more right to security?' Allah reveals the answer: 'Those who believe and do not mix their faith with injustice (Shirk)—they will have security, and they are rightly guided.' That was Our argument which We gave to Ibrahim against his people.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 84–90: The Grand Roster of the 18 Prophets of Guidance\n\nWe gave him Ishaq and Ya'qub. And Nuh We guided before. And of his descendants: Dawud, Sulayman, Ayyub, Yusuf, Musa, Harun; and Zakariyya, Yahya, 'Isa, Ilyas—all of the righteous; and Isma'il, Al-Yasa', Yunus, and Lut—each We preferred over the worlds. 'Those are the ones whom Allah has guided, so from their guidance take an example.'\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances (Gharib al-Qur'an)\n\n- **Azar (ءَازَرَ)**: The father (or paternal uncle) of Prophet Ibrahim who carved and worshipped stone idols.\n- **Kawkab / Qamar / Shams (كَوْكَبًا / قَمَرًا / شَمْسًا)**: A star, the glowing moon, and the blazing sun—the three celestial bodies Ibrahim investigated.\n- **La uhibbu al-Afilin (لَآ أُحِبُّ ٱلْءَافِلِينَ)**: 'I do not love that which sets and disappears'—the foundational principle that a deity must be eternal, not transient.\n- **Wajjahtu wajhiya (وَجَّهْتُ وَجْهِىَ)**: 'I have turned my face toward Him Who created the heavens and the earth, inclining to truth.'\n- **Lam yalbisoo imanahum bi-zulm (لَمْ يَلْبِسُوٓا۟ إِيمَٰنَهُم بِظُلْمٍ)**: 'Those who believe and do not mix their faith with injustice (Shirk)—for them is true security.'\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multi-Tier Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah Al-An'am transform daily character, classical scholars emphasize contextualizing its guidance across life stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** The sun, moon, and stars are creations of Allah; we never worship nature, we only worship the One who made nature.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Use your mind and reason to understand your faith: Prophet Ibrahim asked questions and observed nature to arrive at strong conviction.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** Understand the Hadith of Ibn Mas'ud regarding verse 82: 'Zulm' here means Shirk; pure Tawheed free from superstition gives true mental and emotional security (*Amn*).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Recite the declaration of Ibrahim (6:79) during personal devotion: 'Inni wajjahtu wajhiya lilladhi fatara as-samawati wal-arda hanifa'.**\n2. **Study the lives of the 18 prophets enumerated in verses 84–86 to draw inspiration from their patience.**\n3. **Anchor your family's discussions in rational, evidence-based appreciation of Allah's creative power.**\n",
