    "content": "# Surah Al-An'am (الأنعام) — Part 4 of 5: Verses 91–140\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah Al-An'am (The Cattle)**, covering **Verses 91–140**. This lesson is part of the comprehensive FAHMRISE Qur'an Tafsir curriculum, designed to cultivate deep understanding (Fahm), spiritual elevation (Rise), and principled action.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 6 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة الأنعام |\n| **English Meaning** | The Cattle |\n| **Lesson Part** | Part 4 of 5 |\n| **Verses Covered** | Verses 91–140 (50 verses) |\n| **Classification** | Madani Revelation |\n| **Thematic Focus** | Cosmic Agriculture, Stellar Navigation & Denouncing Superstitions |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Circumstances of Revelation (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nRevealed addressing both Jewish skeptics who claimed God never revealed a book to any mortal, and pagan Qurayshi aristocrats who invented superstitious dietary restrictions on cattle based on animal gender and birth order.\n\nScholars of Tafsir (including Imam al-Wahidi and Imam as-Suyuti) highlight that grasping the historical backdrop and authentic occasions of revelation provides an essential anchor for correctly interpreting divine intent and extracting universal legal and spiritual principles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 91–94: Disrespecting Divine Revelation and the Agonies of Death\n\nThey did not appraise Allah with true appraisal when they said: 'Allah revealed nothing to a human.' Ask: Who revealed the Book that Musa brought? Who is more unjust than one who invents a lie about Allah or says 'I have received revelation' when nothing was revealed? If you could see when wrongdoers are in the agonies of death while angels stretch out their hands: 'Discharge your souls! Today you are awarded the penalty of humiliation.'\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 95–103: The Natural Laboratory: Seeds, Dawns, Stars, and Fruits\n\nAllah splits the seed and fruit stone; He brings the living from the dead. He splits the dawn, makes night for rest, and the sun and moon for calculation. He placed the stars for you to navigate by them through darkness. He sends down rain producing green shoots, dense grain, palm fruit-stalks hanging low, olive gardens, and pomegranates. Look at their fruit when they yield and ripen! Yet they attributed partners to Him among the jinn and invented sons and daughters. Vision perceives Him not, but He perceives all vision; He is the Subtle, the Acquainted.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 104–140: Dismantling Pagan Superstitions and Animal Taboos\n\nDo not insult those they invoke besides Allah, lest they insult Allah out of enmity without knowledge. Even if We sent angels and revived the dead, they would not believe unless Allah willed. Exposing pagan superstitions: dedicating portions of crops to idols and other portions to Allah, burying baby girls alive out of fear of poverty, and declaring certain cattle forbidden to ride or milk. Allah created all cattle as sustenance; eat of what Allah's name has been mentioned over.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances (Gharib al-Qur'an)\n\n- **Faliq al-Habb wan-Nawa (فَالِقُ ٱلْحَبِّ وَٱلنَّوَىٰ)**: The Cleaver of the grain seed and fruit stone—causing life to sprout from dormant matter.\n- **Faliq al-Isbah (فَالِقُ ٱلْإِصْبَاحِ)**: The Cleaver of the dawn—splitting the morning light out of the darkest night.\n- **Husbanan (حُسْبَانًا)**: A precise, mathematically calibrated calculation for time, seasons, and years.\n- **Kharaqu lahu banina wa-banat (خَرَقُوا۟ لَهُۥ بَنِينَ وَبَنَٰتٍۭ)**: They falsely fabricated and falsely attributed to Him sons and daughters without knowledge.\n- **Al-An'am (ٱلْأَنْعَٰمِ)**: Cattle and livestock (camels, cows, sheep, goats)—given as a divine blessing, free from pagan taboos.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multi-Tier Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah Al-An'am transform daily character, classical scholars emphasize contextualizing its guidance across life stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** Look at delicious fruits like dates, olives, and pomegranates on trees; Allah made them grow from tiny seeds for us to eat.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Verse 108 teaches brilliant social etiquette: never insult other people's religious symbols, or they will insult Allah out of anger.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** Avoid all modern superstitions, omens, or attributing un-Islamic taboos to food; Islam liberates humanity from arbitrary pagan restrictions.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Observe the golden rule of interfaith diplomacy (6:108): never mock, insult, or disparage other faiths' deities or sacred objects.**\n2. **Contemplate agricultural miracles (seeds sprouting into tall trees) to deepen your conviction in the resurrection.**\n3. **Recite verse 103 when meditating upon divine majesty: 'La tudrikuhu al-absaru wa-huwa yudriku al-absara wa-huwa al-Latifu al-Khabir'.**\n",
    "practicalApplication": "Incorporate Surah Al-An'am (Verses 91–140) into your daily reflection and voluntary prayers. Focus upon its central theme: Cosmic Agriculture, Stellar Navigation & Denouncing Superstitions. Commit to implementing its practical action steps in your home, studies, and workplace.",
