    "content": "# Surah Al-An'am (الأنعام) — Part 5 of 5: Verses 141–165\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah Al-An'am (The Cattle)**, covering **Verses 141–165**. This lesson is part of the comprehensive FAHMRISE Qur'an Tafsir curriculum, designed to cultivate deep understanding (Fahm), spiritual elevation (Rise), and principled action.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 6 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة الأنعام |\n| **English Meaning** | The Cattle |\n| **Lesson Part** | Part 5 of 5 |\n| **Verses Covered** | Verses 141–165 (25 verses) |\n| **Classification** | Madani Revelation |\n| **Thematic Focus** | The Ten Commandments of the Qur'an & The Grand Creed of Dedication |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Circumstances of Revelation (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nRevealed as the grand ethical culmination of Surah Al-An'am, summarizing the core universal moral and legal duties given to all humanity and echoing the essential commandments of the ancient Abrahamic scrolls.\n\nScholars of Tafsir (including Imam al-Wahidi and Imam as-Suyuti) highlight that grasping the historical backdrop and authentic occasions of revelation provides an essential anchor for correctly interpreting divine intent and extracting universal legal and spiritual principles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 141–150: Agricultural Gratitude and Refuting Fatalism\n\nAllah produced trellised and untrellised gardens, palms, crops of diverse tastes, olives, and pomegranates. Eat of their fruit and give their due on harvest day; do not be wasteful. The polytheists argue fatalistically: 'If Allah willed, we would not have worshipped idols nor our fathers.' That was the lie of earlier deniers. Produce your proof! You follow only conjecture.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 151–153: The Ten Commandments of Islam (Ayat al-Wasaya)\n\nSay: Come, I will recite what your Lord has forbidden to you: (1) Associate nothing with Him; (2) Be good to parents; (3) Do not kill your children out of poverty—We provide for you and them; (4) Do not approach immoralities, open or concealed; (5) Do not kill the soul which Allah made sacred except by legal right; (6) Do not approach the orphan's property except to improve it until maturity; (7) Give full measure and weight with justice; (8) When you testify, be just even if against kin; (9) Fulfill the covenant of Allah; (10) 'And this is My straight path, so follow it, and follow not other paths that divide you from His way.'\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 154–165: The Book of Musa and the Climax Creed of Dedication\n\nThe Book given to Musa was complete. This Qur'an is a blessed Book, so follow it and be God-fearing. He who brings a good deed will have tenfold, and he who brings an evil will be recompensed only like it without injustice. Say: 'Indeed, my Lord has guided me to a straight path—a correct religion, the community of Ibrahim, a Hanif.' Say: 'Indeed, my prayer, my rites of sacrifice, my living and my dying are for Allah, Lord of the worlds. No partner has He. And this I have been commanded, and I am the first of the Muslims.' No soul bears the burden of another; to your Lord is your return.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances (Gharib al-Qur'an)\n\n- **Haqqahu yawma hasadih (حَقَّهُۥ يَوْمَ حَصَادِهِۦ)**: Its due charity and Zakah on the day of harvest, before hoarding or storing.\n- **Ayat al-Wasaya (ءَايَٰتُ ٱلْوَصَايَا)**: The Verses of Solemn Bequests (6:151–153)—the ten eternal ethical commandments of Islam.\n- **Sirati Mustaqeeman (صِرَٰطِى مُسْتَقِيمًا)**: 'This is My Straight Path, so follow it, and do not follow other paths that branch you away from His way.'\n- **La taziru waziratun wizra ukhra (وَلَا تَزِرُ وَازِرَةٌ وِزْرَ أُخْرَىٰ)**: 'No bearer of burdens will bear the burden of another'—absolute individual moral responsibility.\n- **Nusuki wa-Mahyaya wa-Mamati (نُسُكِى وَمَحْيَاىَ وَمَمَاتِى)**: My rites of sacrifice, my entire life, and my death belong exclusively to Allah.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multi-Tier Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah Al-An'am transform daily character, classical scholars emphasize contextualizing its guidance across life stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** Be kind to your parents, share your food, never lie, and always remember that Allah is watching out for you and providing for your family.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Memorize verse 153: there is only one Straight Path; avoid following wild trends and branching lifestyle fads (Subul) that lead you away from God.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** undefined\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Memorize and recite the master creed of verse 162 in your daily prayers: 'Inna salati wa-nusuki wa-mahyaya wa-mamati lillahi Rabbi al-'Alamin'.**\n2. **Live by the Ten Commandments of verses 151–153 as a practical moral code in your household and business.**\n3. **Give agricultural charity or a portion of earned income on the day it arrives, fulfilling 'Wa-atu haqqahu yawma hasadih'.**\n",
