    "content": "# Surah Al-A'raf (الأعراف) — Part 5 of 6: Verses 142–171\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah Al-A'raf (The Heights)**, covering **Verses 142–171**. This lesson is part of the comprehensive FAHMRISE Qur'an Tafsir curriculum, designed to cultivate deep understanding (Fahm), spiritual elevation (Rise), and principled action.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 7 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة الأعراف |\n| **English Meaning** | The Heights |\n| **Lesson Part** | Part 5 of 6 |\n| **Verses Covered** | Verses 142–171 (30 verses) |\n| **Classification** | Madani Revelation |\n| **Thematic Focus** | Mount Sinai, The Shattered Mountain & The Golden Calf |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Circumstances of Revelation (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nRevealed to explain the origins of Jewish legal tradition, the incident of the Sabbath transgression at the coastal town of Aylah, and to emphasize that seeing God in this worldly life is beyond human capacity.\n\nScholars of Tafsir (including Imam al-Wahidi and Imam as-Suyuti) highlight that grasping the historical backdrop and authentic occasions of revelation provides an essential anchor for correctly interpreting divine intent and extracting universal legal and spiritual principles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 142–147: Musa on Mount Sinai and the Shattered Mountain\n\nMusa's appointment extended to 40 nights. Harun is placed in charge. When Musa arrives and Allah speaks to him, Musa says: 'My Lord, show me Yourself that I may look at You.' Allah says: 'You will not see Me, but look at the mountain; if it stays in its place, you will see Me.' When his Lord manifested His glory to the mountain, He made it crumble to dust, and Musa fell unconscious! Waking up, Musa says: 'Glory be to You! I repent to You and I am the first of believers.' Allah gives him the Tablets.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 148–156: The Golden Calf and Musa's Grief and Reproach\n\nIn Musa's absence, the people take ornaments to cast a calf statue with a hollow lowing sound. Did they not see it neither speaks nor guides them? Upon returning, Musa is filled with anger and grief. He throws down the tablets, seizes his brother Harun by his beard. Harun explains: 'Son of my mother, the people overpowered me and almost killed me.' Musa prays for forgiveness. The 70 elders taken to the mountain are struck by an earthquake; Musa pleads for mercy, and Allah answers: 'My punishment I afflict with whom I will, but My mercy encompasses all things.'\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 157–171: The Unlettered Prophet and the Sabbath Transgressors\n\nThe description of the Unlettered Prophet (An-Nabi al-Ummi) found written in their Torah and Gospel: enjoining good, forbidding evil, making lawful pure things, prohibiting harmful things, and relieving their burdens. The coastal town that violated the Sabbath by setting fish nets on Friday evening and collecting on Sunday. The three groups: the sinners, the rebukers, and the apathetic. Allah saved those who forbade evil and punished the transgressors. The mountain raised over them like a dark canopy.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances (Gharib al-Qur'an)\n\n- **Arba'ina laylah (أَرْبَعِينَ لَيْلَةً)**: Forty nights of secluded spiritual communion appointed for Prophet Musa on Mount Sinai.\n- **Lan tarani (لَن تَرَىٰنِى)**: 'You will never see Me [in this worldly life]'—the divine answer when Musa asked to gaze upon Allah.\n- **Ja'alahu dakkan (جَعَلَهُۥ دَكًّا)**: He made the mountain crumble into level powder and crushed dust upon divine manifestation.\n- **Al-Alwah (ٱلْأَلْوَاحِ)**: The Tablets of stone upon which divine legislation and moral admonition were inscribed.\n- **Khurdan Jasadan lahu khuwar (عِجْلًا جَسَدًا لَّهُۥ خُوَارٌ)**: A physical calf statue fashioned from melted gold that emitted a hollow lowing sound.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multi-Tier Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah Al-A'raf transform daily character, classical scholars emphasize contextualizing its guidance across life stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** When someone leaves you in charge of a game or room, be responsible like Prophet Harun, and always forgive your siblings when misunderstandings happen.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Don't look for sneaky 'loopholes' to do bad things, like the Sabbath-breakers who trapped fish on Friday to avoid working on Saturday; Allah knows our real intentions.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** Contemplate the divine statement in verse 156: 'Wa-rahmati wasi'at kulla shay'' (And My mercy encompasses all things); never despair of divine forgiveness, regardless of past sins.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Memorize and live by the majestic description of divine mercy in verse 156: 'Wa-rahmati wasi'at kulla shay''.**\n2. **Avoid technical loopholes designed to bypass Islamic ethical boundaries (like the Sabbath-breakers of Aylah).**\n3. **Honor Prophet Muhammad ﷺ as 'An-Nabi al-Ummi' who freed humanity from heavy burdens and shackles (7:157).**\n",
    "practicalApplication": "Incorporate Surah Al-A'raf (Verses 142–171) into your daily reflection and voluntary prayers. Focus upon its central theme: Mount Sinai, The Shattered Mountain & The Golden Calf. Commit to implementing its practical action steps in your home, studies, and workplace.",
