    "content": "# Surah Al-Anfal (الأنفال) — Part 3 of 3: Verses 55–75\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah Al-Anfal (The Spoils of War)**, covering **Verses 55–75**. This lesson is part of the comprehensive FAHMRISE Qur'an Tafsir curriculum, designed to cultivate deep understanding (Fahm), spiritual elevation (Rise), and principled action.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 8 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة الأنفال |\n| **English Meaning** | The Spoils of War |\n| **Lesson Part** | Part 3 of 3 |\n| **Verses Covered** | Verses 55–75 (21 verses) |\n| **Classification** | Madani Revelation |\n| **Thematic Focus** | Preparedness, The Inclination to Peace & The Bond of Brotherhood |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Circumstances of Revelation (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nRevealed regulating international relations, treaties, and prisoners of war following Badr, establishing that military preparedness is intended to deter aggression, while peace must always be embraced when genuine.\n\nScholars of Tafsir (including Imam al-Wahidi and Imam as-Suyuti) highlight that grasping the historical backdrop and authentic occasions of revelation provides an essential anchor for correctly interpreting divine intent and extracting universal legal and spiritual principles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 55–63: Deterrence Preparedness and the Sincere Embracing of Peace\n\nThe worst creatures in Allah's sight are those who disbelieve and repeatedly break their covenants. Prepare against them whatever force you can muster to deter the enemy of Allah and your enemy. But if they incline to peace, then incline to it also, and place your trust in Allah; He is the Hearing, the Knowing. If they intend to deceive you, Allah is sufficient for you. It is He Who supported you with His help and with the believers, and united their hearts: if you had spent all that is in the earth, you could not have united their hearts, but Allah united them.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 64–71: Ratios of Steadfastness and Rulings on Prisoners\n\nO Prophet, Allah is sufficient for you and whoever follows you of the believers. If there are twenty steadfast among you, they will overcome two hundred; if there are a hundred, they will overcome a thousand. Now Allah has lightened your burden, knowing weakness in you: a hundred steadfast will overcome two hundred. It is not for a prophet to take captives until he has thoroughly subdued the land. Say to the captives in your hands: 'If Allah knows any good in your hearts, He will give you better than what was taken from you and forgive you.'\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 72–75: The Sacred Solidarity: Muhajirun, Ansar, and Kinship\n\nThose who believed, emigrated, and strove with wealth and lives, and those who gave shelter and aided—they are allies of one another. As for those who believed but did not emigrate, you have no duty of protection until they emigrate; but if they seek your help in religion, you must assist them, except against a people with whom you have a treaty. The disbelievers are allies of one another; if you do not do the same (maintain Islamic solidarity), there will be discord on earth and great corruption. Relatives by blood have priority over one another in the Book of Allah.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances (Gharib al-Qur'an)\n\n- **Wa-a'iddu lahum ma astata'tum min quwwah (وَأَعِدُّوا۟ لَهُم مَّا ٱسْتَطَعْتُم مِّن قُوَّةٍ)**: 'And prepare against them whatever you are able of strength and steeds of war'—the doctrine of military deterrence.\n- **Wa-in janahu lis-salm (وَإِن جَنَحُوا۟ لِلسَّلْمِ)**: 'And if they incline to peace, then incline to it also, and rely upon Allah.'\n- **Allafa bayna qulubihim (أَلَّفَ بَيْنَ قُلُوبِهِمْ)**: 'He united their hearts'—a feat no amount of worldly gold could achieve, accomplished only by divine grace.\n- **Sabirun (صَٰبِرُونَ)**: Steadfast, enduring combatants whose faith amplifies their effectiveness against larger numbers.\n- **Muhajirun wa-Ansar (مُهَٰجِرُونَ وَأَنصَارٌ)**: The Emigrants who sacrificed homeland and wealth, and the Helpers who gave shelter and aid—allies of one another.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multi-Tier Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah Al-Anfal transform daily character, classical scholars emphasize contextualizing its guidance across life stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** If a friend wants to make peace and stop fighting, always shake hands, say 'I forgive you', and be friends again.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Real strength prevents fights: when a nation or community is strong and disciplined, bullies don't attack; be strong so you can protect peace.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** Live by the balance of verses 60 and 61: maintain strong professional and civic deterrence, but immediately embrace peace and diplomacy whenever offered.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Always accept genuine offers of reconciliation and peace in family or communal disputes (8:61).**\n2. **Ponder verse 63: human diplomacy and money alone cannot unite broken hearts; pray to Allah to reconcile alienated hearts.**\n3. **Support the principle of mutual alliance by aiding displaced and oppressed brothers and sisters worldwide (8:72).**\n",
    "practicalApplication": "Incorporate Surah Al-Anfal (Verses 55–75) into your daily reflection and voluntary prayers. Focus upon its central theme: Preparedness, The Inclination to Peace & The Bond of Brotherhood. Commit to implementing its practical action steps in your home, studies, and workplace.",
