    "content": "# Surah At-Tawbah (التوبة) — Part 4 of 5: Verses 73–106\n\nWelcome to the verse-by-verse Tafsir of **Surah At-Tawbah (The Repentance)**, covering **Verses 73–106**. This lesson is part of the comprehensive FAHMRISE Qur'an Tafsir curriculum, designed to cultivate deep understanding (Fahm), spiritual elevation (Rise), and principled action.\n\n| Dimension | Details |\n|---|---|\n| **Surah Number** | 9 of 114 |\n| **Arabic Title** | سورة التوبة |\n| **English Meaning** | The Repentance |\n| **Lesson Part** | Part 4 of 5 |\n| **Verses Covered** | Verses 73–106 (34 verses) |\n| **Classification** | Madani Revelation |\n| **Thematic Focus** | The Charter of Zakah Recipients, Striving Against Falsehood & The Scandal of Masjid ad-Dirar |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Circumstances of Revelation (Asbab an-Nuzul)\n\nVerse 60 was revealed to clarify that Zakah is not distributed according to personal whim or tribal favoritism, but strictly by divine ordinance; verses 107–108 were revealed when twelve hypocrites built a rival building near Quba to divide Muslims and spy for Abu 'Amir ar-Rahib, asking the Prophet ﷺ to bless it.\n\nScholars of Tafsir (including Imam al-Wahidi and Imam as-Suyuti) highlight that grasping the historical backdrop and authentic occasions of revelation provides an essential anchor for correctly interpreting divine intent and extracting universal legal and spiritual principles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Verse-by-Verse Exegesis (Tafsir)\n\n### Verses 73–89: Broken Pledges, Hypocritical Envy, and the Loss of Salvation\n\nO Prophet, strive against the disbelievers and the hypocrites and be firm upon them. Their refuge is Hell. Among them are those who made a covenant with Allah: 'If He gives us from His bounty, we will surely give charity and be among the righteous.' But when He gave them of His bounty, they were stingy with it and turned away. So He penalized them with hypocrisy in their hearts until the Day they meet Him, because they broke their promise to Allah and because they were lying. Whether you ask forgiveness for them or do not ask forgiveness for them—even if you ask forgiveness for them seventy times—Allah will not forgive them, because they disbelieved in Allah and His Messenger. Those who stayed behind rejoiced in remaining behind the Messenger of Allah; let them laugh a little and weep much as a recompense for what they used to earn. Never pray over any of them who dies, nor stand at his grave; indeed they disbelieved in Allah and His Messenger and died while defiantly disobedient.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 90–100: The Excused, the Bedouins, and the First Vanguard of Believers\n\nThose with genuine excuses among the desert dwellers came asking for exemption, while those who lied to Allah and His Messenger sat behind. There is no blame upon the weak, nor upon the ill, nor upon those who find nothing to spend, when they are sincere to Allah and His Messenger. There is no cause of blame against the doers of good. Nor is there blame upon those who, when they came to you that you might provide mounts for them, you said: 'I can find nothing for you to ride upon', and they turned back while their eyes overflowed with tears of grief that they could not find anything to spend. The desert dwellers are severest in disbelief and hypocrisy, but among them are those who believe in Allah and the Last Day and consider what they spend as means of nearness to Allah and prayers of the Messenger. The first vanguard of the Muhajirun and the Ansar and those who followed them in goodness—Allah is well pleased with them and they are well pleased with Him, and He has prepared for them gardens beneath which rivers flow to abide therein forever.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n### Verses 101–106: Recognizing Sins, Purifying Wealth, and Masjid ad-Dirar Exposed\n\nAmong the desert dwellers around you are hypocrites, and from the people of Madinah they have become skilled in hypocrisy. Others have acknowledged their sins; they have mixed a righteous deed with another that was bad; perhaps Allah will turn to them in forgiveness. Take from their wealth a charity by which you purify them and cause them increase, and invoke blessings upon them; your invocation is reassurance for them. Do they not know that it is Allah Who accepts repentance from His servants and receives charities? Those who took a mosque for causing harm (Masjid ad-Dirar), disbelief, division among believers, and as an outpost for whoever warred against Allah and His Messenger before—they will swear: 'We intended nothing but good.' But Allah bears witness that they are liars. Do not stand to pray in it ever. A mosque founded on Taqwa from the first day (Masjid Quba) is more worthy that you stand in it.\n\n* **Linguistic Depth & Scholarly Exegesis (Ibn Kathir, As-Sa'di, Akhsaru Tafaseer):** Classical commentators emphasize that these verses establish divine guidance, moral accountability, and spiritual elevation, providing clear criteria for personal ethics and communal governance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Essential Arabic Vocabulary & Linguistic Nuances (Gharib al-Qur'an)\n\n- **As-Sadaqatu lil-fuqara' (ٱلصَّدَقَـٰتُ لِلْفُقَرَآءِ)**: 'Charitable zakah is exclusively for the poor'—the opening of the divine 8-category charter in 9:60.\n- **Masakin (ٱلْمَسَـٰكِينِ)**: The needy whose basic daily income falls short of their essential living costs.\n- **Al-Gharimin (ٱلْغَـٰرِمِينَ)**: Debtors crushed by legitimate burdens incurred without sinful extravagance.\n- **Fi sabilillah (فِى سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ)**: 'In the cause of Allah'—righteous defense, education, and institutional propagation of faith.\n- **Masjidan diraran (مَسْجِدًا ضِرَارًا)**: 'A mosque of harm'—built with malicious intent to cause sectarian division and espionage.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Multi-Tier Practical Guidance Across All Stages of Life\n\nTo ensure the teachings of Surah At-Tawbah transform daily character, classical scholars emphasize contextualizing its guidance across life stages:\n\n* 🧒 **For Children & Young Learners:** When you make a promise to Allah or to your family, keep it faithfully so you don't become like those who broke their vows.\n* 🎒 **For Teenagers & Students:** Look at the poor companions who cried because they couldn't afford to join the expedition: true devotion is measured by heart and sincere tears, not wallet size.\n* 🏛️ **For Adults & Parents:** Memorize the eight zakah categories in verse 9:60; zakah is a divine right of specific vulnerable groups, not discretionary charity.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Action Steps for Daily Life (Tadabbur wa 'Amal)\n\nTrue contemplation (*Tadabbur*) must always culminate in righteous action (*'Amal*):\n\n1. **Study verse 9:60 to identify who qualifies for Zakah in your local community, ensuring payments reach verified recipients.**\n2. **Support mosques built upon sincere Taqwa, unity, and genuine community service, avoiding sectarian factionalism.**\n3. **Give charity promptly upon receiving any unexpected income or bonus, fulfilling vows made in moments of need.**\n",
    "practicalApplication": "Incorporate Surah At-Tawbah (Verses 73–106) into your daily reflection and voluntary prayers. Focus upon its central theme: The Charter of Zakah Recipients, Striving Against Falsehood & The Scandal of Masjid ad-Dirar. Commit to implementing its practical action steps in your home, studies, and workplace.",
